{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Traces are an artifact-storage decision too</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Traces have to actually leave the CI runner to be useful — upload the <code>trace.zip</code> as a build artifact in your CI config, and link it from the failed job's summary so nobody has to go spelunking through logs to find it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>on-first-retry</code> needs at least one retry configured (<code>retries: 1</code> or more) to ever fire — a common gotcha is turning on tracing and seeing no traces show up, because retries are still at the default of 0. <code>retain-on-failure</code> doesn't have that dependency, at the cost of recording (and then discarding) a trace for every passing test too.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Trace files aren't small — a busy suite can produce gigabytes of them across a CI run, so most teams set a retention policy (days, not indefinitely) and upload only the traces for actually-failed tests rather than every retry attempt. It's also worth recording traces for tests you're actively trying to de-flake even when they're not failing outright, via <code>trace: 'on'</code> temporarily scoped to that one file — you get the full timeline for a test that's merely slow or borderline, not just one that hard-fails.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

